WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.410
I have something just really important for
you today, but it also happens to be entertaining

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:06.410
and I think [inaudible]

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.370
most of you are going to enjoy this. There's
a company called Salesforce. We've used this

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:16.119
company off and on to manage our ad sales
process. It's a CRM, a customer relationship

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.090
management tool, and it doesn't even really
matter what that is, but their CEO is a guy

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:27.290
named Mark Benioff and he's a smart guy. He
has some really progressive ideas, at least

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:33.020
in the context of someone who runs a huge
corporation and happens to be a multibillionaire,

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.290
reportedly worth more than six point $4 billion.
About two thirds of that in Salesforce company

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:45.180
stock. He has a narrative about capitalism,
about the role of big business that is pretty

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.290
different from what a lot of other big corporate
CEOs have and he's been talking for a while

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:58.730
now about a new capitalism, a more social
capitalism, capitalism without going off the

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:04.180
rails, so to speak, a capitalism that accounts
for the wellbeing, not just of the shareholders

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:10.230
as is often the case, but also of stakeholders
which include all employees, customers, the

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.789
environment.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:18.369
Capitalism for social change rather than capitalism
for preventing social change. And he was interviewed

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:24.359
on CNBC by the usual suspects, including this
guy named Jim Cramer, who's known in the investment

00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:30.450
world and he turned their world visibly upside
down and they didn't know what to do with

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.990
him. So we're going to look at some clips.
This is a 12 minute interview in total, which

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.359
you can find online. I'm going to play a few
clips for you. And throughout this you might

00:01:38.359 --> 00:01:43.489
notice there will be times where the camera
is a wide shot. And you'll notice Jim Cramer

00:01:43.489 --> 00:01:48.239
just looking exasperated with Mark Benioff,
who's not going to be bullied by the sort

00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:53.439
of vulture capitalist mentality into saying
anything other than what Mark Benioff believes.

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.340
And he happens to believe many of the right
things. And you'll see Jim Cramer going on

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.590
him about are we really going to break up
big tech, which Elizabeth Warren has been

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.569
suggesting. And Mark Benioff says, yeah, yeah
we should.

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:12.159
Last night I heard capitalism under attack
as I never heard it before. And it made me

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:16.040
think that if it's dead, there are two visions.
There's your vision, which is to resurrect

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.930
it and make business the greatest Pratt platform,
social change, and there's another vision

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.880
which is that the government has to step in
because the billionaires are screwed up so

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.580
badly. Tell us how to get that. Not that blow.
They're probably right actually, but we do.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:35.120
We need a new capitalism. We need a capitalism
that's more fair, more equitable, more sustainable

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.430
capitalism, capitalism that

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:43.230
values not just all shareholders but also
all stake holders and if we can put those

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.859
things together, I think it can be a new capitalism
to be very exciting for everybody.

00:02:46.859 --> 00:02:54.000
I I do worry Mark that in the, in the vacuum,
in the vacuum of what you're talking about,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.989
what I heard last night about big tech having
to be broken up simply because it's got tremendous

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:04.390
power, not even at the power's being used
unwisely, but where are you when it comes

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.879
down to breaking up Facebook, waking up Amazon,
breaking up alphabet. Aren't these great American

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:10.689
companies that hire a lot of people?

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:15.140
Well, you know how I feel gym. Facebook is
the new cigarettes. It's addictive. Bad for

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.650
you, Jim. Thereafter, your kids set's not
fanned off, changed the political ads that

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.560
are going on. There's, you know, not a commitment
to trust and truth and now they're co-mingling

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.019
the data of all of their acquisitions. Facebook
probably should be broken up because they're

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.579
using these assets in a way to further manipulate
even more people. I think that there needs

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:38.830
to be change. It hasn't happened fast enough.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:46.599
Absolutely correct. He's a huge beneficiary
of the system that we have, but he's saying

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:52.560
plain and simple that the system still has
to be changed and Kramer pushes back because

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.870
he just doesn't, I mean he doesn't like it.
He can't believe that a multibillionaire,

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:01.489
a huge corporate CEO is saying this stuff
and Mark Benioff here to the disbelief of

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:07.650
the people on the panel explaining that Salesforce
has returned great value to investors. Investors

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:14.620
in Salesforce have done really well financially,
but that they're also providing a return to

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.320
stakeholders and focusing on the environment
and more.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.120
Mark, you've been talking for some time now
about, and that's the new capitalism gym,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.130
which is that, look, we have to actually value
all stakeholders. We are. Look at all shit.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:32.669
We'll share with the earlier [inaudible] are
important and we've had a great shareholder

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:37.830
return at Salesforce. I heard you earlier
mentioned at 3500% since I was first year

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:45.590
right there, right there in 2004 if you bought
our stock, well that's a 3500% return, but

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.940
I had a great stakeholder return too. During
that same period, we've given over $300 million

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:56.190
for grants, 4 million hours of volunteerism,
40,000 nonprofits and NGOs for free running

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:02.580
on our service, and we're a net zero company,
be fully renewable by 2025 that's our stakeholder

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.030
return. We issue a stakeholder report, so
we have a shareholder return and a shareholder

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:12.720
report. We have a stakeholder return, a stakeholder
report, and I think that's something the sec

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.310
can do by the way. They could put, you know
I mentioned that in my op ed.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.411
What a concept, right? I mean blowing their
minds. You can see that these people, they're

00:05:20.411 --> 00:05:24.379
looking at each other, they're smirking, they're
rolling their eyes. They can't believe that

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:30.360
a company that makes money also treats the
planet in a more responsible way and it treats

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:36.129
stakeholders as an important part of the considerations
that are made when you make business decisions.

00:05:36.129 --> 00:05:41.879
Now, disclaimer, time. I'm not saying Mark
Benioff is perfect. I'm not saying Salesforce

00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:47.060
is the perfect company and there are no negative
externalities to what Salesforce does there

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:53.300
well may be, but the way in which these simple
socially democratic ideas are coming from

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:59.660
a multibillionaire CEO and triggering Jim
Kramer is just delightful. Watch Kramer in

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.949
this next clip, if you're watching, if you're
just listening, I encourage you to find the

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:08.780
full interview online while David Faber takes
a turn asking some questions of Mark Benioff.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.140
Jim Cramer looks perplexed as Mark Benioff
explains, Hey, there are actually CEOs all

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.930
over the world being more thoughtful about
how we run our businesses.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:23.099
What are the metrics? How do you measure this
given it is growing in importance. We hear

00:06:23.099 --> 00:06:29.190
about it all the time now from real money,
not just sort of a, you know, a broad sense

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:34.150
that there's interest in it, but people actually
who want to put dollars behind them. Well,

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:38.060
my peers certainly do. When I talked to the
largest CEOs in the world yesterday, I was

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:43.280
in Munich, Germany with one of the largest
company in Germany with their CEO. They have

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:49.849
a major program into place just as we do just
as many others do. I was in London on Monday.

00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:54.410
I see the same thing throughout Europe. It's
probably happening faster there. The United

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:59.460
nations sustainable development goals are
the 17 core initiatives that are laid out

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:04.620
by the UN of how we're trying to improve the
state of the world and companies should have

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.409
to report how they're doing against those
goals that that just makes complete sense

00:07:09.409 --> 00:07:10.409
to me.

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:14.331
If we're not all into improving the state
of the world, then what are we all into? Did

00:07:14.331 --> 00:07:18.879
you believe the business round table when
they changed their statement or do you think

00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:22.560
as some do that it was really more than just
kind of talked to get on the right side of

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.810
the politics of it? The business round table
has said now exactly the same thing. The purpose

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:35.110
of the corporation is to value stakeholders
as important as shareholders that the traditional

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.570
Milton Friedman concept that that it's only
about the shareholder, that the CEO's only

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:42.879
job is to maximize shareholder return is dead.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:50.550
Wow. Oversight regulation working together.
Milton Friedman's teachings are obsolete.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:57.050
It's not only about maximizing shareholder
value. Socialism, no, this is not socialism.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:02.050
This is social democracy. It's social capitalism.
Mark Benioff doesn't say big corporations

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:07.030
shouldn't exist. Mark Benioff doesn't say
billionaires like him shouldn't exist. He

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:15.280
is saying that we can do capitalism in a different
way, still make money, but consider all stakeholders.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:21.590
Consider the environment. This is CNBC. Mind
you, this is a billionaire tech CEO and you'll

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:26.550
see the wide shot again. Show Jim Kramer who
was just laughing to himself. He can't believe

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.710
this is happening on CNBC. He's here.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:33.230
We need to value all stakeholders and all
shareholders. Now, when I say stakeholders,

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:38.690
what do I mean? I mean our employees, our
customers, our partners, you know, but I also

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:42.650
mean like I'm in from San Francisco. We have
a horrible homeless problem there. The homeless

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:47.840
are a stakeholder for me. That's why I was
fighting for proposition C, a tax on ourself

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.240
because we have to get to, we have to deal
with the homeless situation in San Francisco

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.430
as the largest employer in San Francisco.
I'm not focused on that if they aren't one

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.290
of my key stakeholders than what is,

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:02.820
that was the question I had because at tech
crunch you said if your orientation is just

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:07.060
about making money, you're not going to hang
out for very long. As a CEO or founder of

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:12.531
the company. My question was, if you don't,
then what you, your board fires you, your

00:09:12.531 --> 00:09:17.230
sales drop, you know you can't hire anybody.
What is the consequence of running a foul

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:18.230
of that?

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:23.020
Well, you can see that in my industry, which
is for CEOs who do not take this approach,

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:28.230
they are really at risk and they can be extremely
vulnerable, especially during economic times

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.370
that become turbulent,

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:36.851
stunning, right? Who could have imagined?
You could be a corporate, a CEO or a big corporation

00:09:36.851 --> 00:09:41.510
and care about other people. Then Benioff
brings up climate change, which is just like,

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.190
it's lost on these folks and you'll see them
all smirk at each other.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:49.980
What am I doing wrong? I need to be thinking
about the public schools, the homeless. Oh,

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.410
and I also need to be thinking about not just
profit, but planet. I need to be thinking

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:58.700
about the planet. What are my emissions? You
know, that is part of the new world we're

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:03.120
in. That gets back to David's question on
what about the sustainable development goals

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.820
in the UN? That is the connectivity. That's
the map. We can lay it out. Then we can measure.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.000
If we can measure it, we can have impact.
That's our one that we all know that we understand

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.030
how to do that. Jim, I know has to finish,
but there's some shareholders right now or

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:20.730
listening to you who probably like what are
you talking about? You seem to be abandoning

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:27.070
what I would want, which is just you focus
on improving the profitability of this company.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.270
You worry that you freak some of these people
out and what do they say to you when they

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:36.360
hear that if they're not onboard with your
view, this is how to improve the profitability

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.170
of a company. This is how to make a great
company. This is how to have a company that

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:45.730
customers want to do business with, that attracts
employees, that develops partnerships, that

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:51.700
integrates local communities, that saves the
planet. This is about having a great company

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:56.480
and look at our company. Look at our financial
return since we went public right here in

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:01.980
2004 look at how we rank against everyone
else over the last same 15 year period. I'm

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:06.911
sure that you'll see we're in the very top
echelon and I'll tell you how we did it. We

00:11:06.911 --> 00:11:11.230
valued stakeholders as much as shareholders.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:16.990
Consider that you improve the company by caring
about the world in which the company exists.

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:24.060
By caring about the people who could be employees
or potential employees, customers of the company,

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:30.240
just people who exist on this planet. Shocking,
right? And half of what makes this great is

